        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mary, mother of Jesus</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1st century BCE – 1st century CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">Date not recorded; a Jewish woman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, betrothed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-joseph">Joseph</a>; the Gospel of James, a later text, names her parents as Joachim and Anne</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">Not recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>; Hyppolitus of Thebes, writing in the 7th or 8th century, says she died in 41 CE; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> tradition holds that she died naturally (the Dormition) and was then taken bodily into Heaven</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-joseph">Joseph</a>; held by Eastern and Oriental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Catholic, Lutheran, Reformed, Anglican and Methodist churches to be the Mother of God; historians know her chiefly through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> and Acts</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the annunciation by the angel Gabriel; the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>; presence at the wedding at Cana and at the crucifixion; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnificat">Magnificat</a>; Theotokos, proclaimed at the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> in 431; the Assumption; the only woman named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a></span></div>
