    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> of Matthew and Luke describe Mary as a virgin chosen by God to conceive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> through the Holy Spirit, and they place her at the birth of Jesus, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a>, and at his crucifixion.</li><li>Little is recorded of her life: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> does not describe her death, and the later accounts of her parents, childhood and end are scholars' and churches' traditions rather than firm history.</li><li>Churches differ widely over her: Catholics and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> hold distinctive beliefs and devotions, most Protestants honour her without venerating her, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> gives her a chapter of her own.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Mary is the only woman named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, which mentions or refers to her 50 times and gives one chapter her name, while the earliest known prayer to her, the Sub tuum praesidium, was found on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1917.</p></aside>
    </section>

