    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 6 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Mary, betrothed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-joseph">Joseph</a>, is told by the angel Gabriel that she will conceive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> through the Holy Spirit, as Luke tells it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 5 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">She gives birth to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, according to Luke during a Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and presents him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 28 CE</span><span class="rf-text">At her suggestion <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> works his first miracle at the wedding at Cana, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">30 CE</span><span class="rf-text">She stands near the cross at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crucifixion-of-jesus">crucifixion of Jesus</a>, in John's account</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 30 CE</span><span class="rf-text">Acts names her with the apostles and the brothers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in the upper room after the Ascension</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">431</span><span class="rf-text">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> declares her Theotokos, the God-bearer</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1854</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> proclaims the Immaculate Conception</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1950</span><span class="rf-text">Pope Pius XII defines the Assumption in Munificentissimus Deus</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke.jpg" alt="Gospel of Luke" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luke_13.29-35_and_14.10_(CBL_BP_I,_f.15r).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> tells little of Mary's early history. Her name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> Miryam, rendered Mariam or Maria in Greek. The earliest mention is in the letter to the Galatians, written before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a>, which speaks of God's Son as born of a woman without naming her. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a> calls her a relative of Elizabeth, the wife of the priest Zechariah, and Matthew gives a genealogy only for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-joseph">Joseph</a>, her husband. The Gospel of James, a later text that scholars generally treat as historically unreliable, gives her parents as Joachim and Anne and says she was given to service in the Temple at three years old. Some of those who think her relationship with Elizabeth was on her mother's side hold that Mary too was of the royal line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, though Matthew and Luke give no such genealogy for her.</p><p>The Gospels place her at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, betrothed to Joseph, the first stage of a Jewish marriage. Jewish girls were considered marriageable at twelve and a half, though the actual age varied. Scholars hold that Jewish girls typically married between the mid teens and early twenties, and the Gospels do not give her age. The Gospels were composed from about 66 to 110 CE, and historians note that early Christian writings often mixed historical fact with legend.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-joseph"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-joseph.jpg" alt="Saint Joseph" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-joseph">Saint Joseph</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guido_Reni_-_St_Joseph_with_the_Infant_Jesus_-_WGA19304.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Guido Reni, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a> the angel Gabriel tells Mary that she is to conceive the promised Messiah through the Holy Spirit. After some questioning she answers, in Luke's words, "I am the handmaid of the Lord. Let it be done unto me according to your word." Matthew adds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-joseph">Joseph</a> planned to divorce her quietly until an angel told him in a dream that the child was conceived by the Holy Spirit, and he took her as his wife. Christians hold that she remained a virgin while conceiving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, a belief that was almost universal among Christians from the 2nd to the 19th century.</p><p>Luke tells that Mary hurried to Elizabeth, who greeted her as the mother of her Lord. Mary then spoke the words of praise that became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnificat">Magnificat</a>, and she returned home after about three months. A decree of Augustus brought her and Joseph to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, where she gave birth to Jesus and used a manger as a cradle. Jesus was circumcised after eight days, and after 40 days she brought her offering to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a>. According to Matthew the family fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from Herod and settled again in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a> after Herod's death in 4 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jesus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jesus.jpg" alt="Jesus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spas_vsederzhitel_sinay_(cropped1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mary appears in few scenes of the ministry. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> was twelve, Luke tells, he stayed behind in the Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> and she found him among the teachers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a> places her at the wedding at Cana, where at her suggestion Jesus performs his first miracle by turning water into wine. John calls him addressing her as Woman, which in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a> was not disrespectful and could be tender. It is the only passage in which the adult Jesus speaks with her.</p><p>She is mentioned along with his brothers, whose identity is disputed. Epiphanius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a> and Eusebius held that they were sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-joseph">Joseph</a> by an earlier marriage, which remains the position of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> held that they were cousins, the Roman Catholic position, while Helvidius held that they were full siblings, the commonest Protestant view. John places her at the crucifixion, near the disciple whom Jesus loved. Acts 1:14 names her as the only woman other than the apostles in the upper room after the Ascension. The Book of Revelation's woman clothed with the sun is sometimes identified as Mary.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/origen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/origen.jpg" alt="Origen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Origen3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> record no persecution of Mary herself. They do record friction within her family: Mark says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>'s mother and brothers came to take him because people said he was out of his mind, and Jesus answers that those who do the will of God are his mother and brothers. The Gospels also show <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-joseph">Joseph</a> fearing her pregnancy and Herod seeking the child.</p><p>Belief in her virginity was disputed early. In the 2nd century the pagan philosopher Celsus, in The True Word, contended that Jesus was the illegitimate son of a Roman soldier named Panthera, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a> dismissed this as a complete fabrication in Against Celsus. The Toledot Yeshu, a medieval Jewish text that circulated widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> from the 9th century, told the story of Panthera and gave Jewish readers a negative picture of Mary; scholars such as Raymond E. Brown and Robert Van Voorst consider it a late and fanciful source with little historical value. After a disputation in 1240 copies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> were burnt on a court order for allegedly defaming her.</p><p>Biblical scholars still debate what Matthew 1:25 means by "until", and whether the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> almah, a young woman, was rightly rendered by the Greek parthenos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> 7:14. Bart Ehrman is among those who note the difference. Matthew and Luke give differing versions of the birth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pentecost"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pentecost.jpg" alt="Pentecost" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pentecost">Pentecost</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ingeborg_Psalter_02f_1200_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Her death is not recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. Acts leaves her with the apostles after the Ascension, and Christian tradition holds that she was present at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentecost">Pentecost</a>, though the text does not say so. John says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> asked the disciple whom he loved to look after her, and Irenaeus and Eusebius wrote that John later went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, which may be the basis of the early belief that she lived there. A house at Ephesus is now venerated, a claim made in the 19th century from the visions of Anne Catherine Emmerich.</p><p>Hyppolitus of Thebes, in the 7th or 8th century, wrote that Mary lived 11 years after the death of her son and died in 41 CE. The earliest surviving biography of her, the Life of the Virgin, is attributed to Maximus the Confessor in the 7th century. The Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> hold that she died naturally, an event called the Dormition, and was then taken bodily into Heaven; Eastern Christians believe she was laid in a tomb near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> first. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> defines the Assumption as dogma in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Eastern Churches alike, without defining whether she died, and it is believed also by the Oriental Orthodox and parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglicanism">Anglican Communion</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix.jpg" alt="Pope Pius IX" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pius_IX,_by_Adolphe_Braun,_1875.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mary has been venerated since early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. The Sub tuum praesidium, the oldest known prayer to her, dates to about 250 or 270 CE. The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> in 431 declared her Theotokos, the God-bearer, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> is held to be one person who is both God and man. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> proclaimed the Immaculate Conception in 1854, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> do not accept because their understanding of ancestral sin differs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> holds four Marian dogmas, the Mother of God, perpetual virginity, the Immaculate Conception and the Assumption, and it keeps more feasts and prayers to her than any other group, among them the Rosary. The Orthodox honour her above the angels in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akathist">Akathist Hymn</a>.</p><p>Protestants generally reject the invocation of saints. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> honoured her, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> granted her the highest honour while denying that she can intercede. Anglicans differ among themselves, Methodists hold the virgin birth, and nontrinitarian groups acknowledge her as Jesus's mother without the title Mother of God.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, Maryam is the daughter of Imran, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> calls her chosen above the women of the world, and a chapter is named for her. Muslims believe in the virgin birth of Jesus, born through the Spirit. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> and the Baháʼí Faith also revere her. She is traditionally painted in blue, and the Council of Ephesus and the Roman feast days still shape the Christian year.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mary was a Jewish woman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a>, the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> of Matthew and Luke describe as a virgin. She is venerated across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and holds the highest place among women in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, while her death is not recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>.</em></p>

